<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sas-Bahu Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sahastrabahu Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sas-Bahu Temple is a pair of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort">Gwalior Fort</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and completed in 1093 by the Kachchhapaghata king Mahipala. The larger temple, though its tower has collapsed, is richly carved inside and out. The popular name, &quot;mother-in-law and daughter-in-law&quot;, derives from Sahastrabahu, a name of Vishnu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sas-bahu-temple.jpg" alt="Sas-Bahu Temple" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Airunp · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sas_Bahu_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The twin temples stand within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort">Gwalior Fort</a> complex and are also known as the Sas Bahu Twin Temples or Sas aur Bahu ke Mandir. According to an inscription found in the larger temple, King Mahipala of the Kachchhapaghata dynasty built them between 1090 and 1093. The inscription was deciphered by Alexander Cunningham, the first Director-General of the Archaeological Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Like most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Jain temples in the region, the pair fell largely into ruin, badly damaged in numerous invasions and wars. Only the larger temple, the Sas, survives in some form. The smaller Bahu temple is a shell of its original single storey, keeping a highly ornate door frame and defaced wall reliefs, and its remains suggest it may have been a smaller version of its companion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The larger temple stands on a jagati platform 100 feet (30 m) long and 63 feet (19 m) wide. It was built in three storeys, one of its distinguishing features, and the scholar Adam Hardy describes it as following a central cluster concept. Its tower and sanctum are lost; the entrance porch and the mandapa survive. James Harle argued that the triple-storey plan on a cruciform foundation, with balconies, points to the North Indian Bhumija style.</p><p>The Sas temple has a square sanctum joined to a two-storey antarala and a closed three-storey mandapa with three entrances. Four load-bearing pillars in the Ruchaka ghatapallava style carry the main porch. Inside the lintels are friezes of scenes from the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, while the outer faces tell legends from other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> texts, and above them is Garuda, the mount of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. Carvings, now much defaced, cover all the exterior walls and every surviving interior surface, and the pillars bear carvings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shaktism</a>.</p><p>The Bahu temple has a square sanctum 9.33 feet (2.84 m) a side with four central pillars, and a square maha-mandapa 23.33 feet (7.11 m) a side with twelve pillars. Its roof is formed of two rotated squares that intersect as an octagon, capped by overlapping circles. The sanctum holds a damaged image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, holding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, on one side and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, holding the trident, on the other.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sasbahu Temple, Gwalior” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mahipala completed the great <b>Sas-Bahu</b> temple at Gwalior fort in 1093, and the Dubkund branch built Jain temples, showing patronage of both faiths.</blockquote><a href="/state/kachchhapaghata-dynasty">Kachchhapaghata dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
